[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
Router0 has not stopped routing. Evidence on router0 shows it is operating correctly: IP forwarding is enabled (net.ipv4.ip_forward=1), there are no blocking firewall rules (iptables policies ACCEPT), and RIP has learned routes to both the Server Room (R>* 1.0.0.0/8) and Internet Lab (R>* 128.168.0.0/16) via 10.10.0.2. From router0, 10.10.0.2 (router1) is reachable, but traceroutes from IT/CS hosts to 1.0.0.5/128.168.0.2 go 192.168.1.1/192.168.2.1 -> 10.10.0.2 and then stall, localizing the break past router0 at router1.

On router1, the forwarding path is being dropped by a blanket firewall rule: iptables shows “-A FORWARD -j DROP” and the FORWARD chain counters are incrementing on the DROP rule. This rule drops all transit traffic through router1, explaining why:
- IT/CS cannot reach Server Room or Internet Lab (their packets must transit router1 and get dropped).
- Router1 can still ping those networks (locally originated traffic uses OUTPUT, not FORWARD).
- Router2 cannot ping back to IT/CS (its return packets are also dropped at router1).

Root cause: a misconfigured iptables rule on router1 that unconditionally drops forwarded traffic.

Corrective fix: remove or supersede the catch‑all DROP in FORWARD on router1. For example, delete it (iptables -D FORWARD 1 or iptables -F FORWARD) and, if needed, replace with appropriate ACCEPT rules (e.g., allow established/related and inter-router subnets) and set policy to ACCEPT. Persist the corrected rules so they survive reloads. After this, router0-based clients will reach 1.0.0.x and 128.168.0.x again. 
=== END ===